At .eco, part of our mission is to help everyone make more **eco-friendly technology choices**. One of the most environmentally impactful decisions that you make when setting up a new website is [choosing where to host it](/news/where-you-host-your-website-really-matters/). That’s why whenever someone [searches for a .eco domain](/search/) on this site, we highlight domain retailers and web hosts that [operate sustainably](/news/eco-friendly-web-host/), by running on renewable energy, having high energy-efficiency or publishing clear environmental policies and commitments.

To understand how the .eco community is following this guidance, we annually review all .eco domains to see how many are using sustainable web hosting.

This is our fifth year doing this analysis. Every year, we've gained new insights by reviewing this data and we're happy to share what we've learned.

### Approach

To perform this analysis, we first identify every .eco domain with an active website. We define an active .eco website as:
- resolving successfully (returning a 200 HTTP status code)
- being served from a .eco domain (does not redirect to a domain name under a different TLD)
- containing meaningful content (it is not a registrar parking or contruction page or is otherwise devoid of useful information) 

Once we determine the set of .eco domain name with active websites, we check which of these domains are using eco-friendly web hosting by using [Green Web Check](https://www.thegreenwebfoundation.org/green-web-check/) from the Green Web Foundation.

### 2026 Results

As of September 24, 2026, **46% of active .eco websites** use an eco-friendly web hosting provider. This is an increase of 1% from last year.

.eco domains use **81** distinct green web hosting providers. This is a drop of 14 from last year. The Green Web Foundation has [steadily strengthened the criteria](https://www.thegreenwebfoundation.org/news/a-summary-of-the-upcoming-changes-to-our-verification-criteria/) that they use to determine if a hosting provider's claims as qualify. We will discuss the implications of these changes later in this post.

