Some of the largest hosting providers removed from the Green Hosting Directory this year include:
- Microsoft Azure (1% of last year's websites)
- World4You (Austria)

There were also some notable hosting providers added (or re-added) to the Green Hosting Directory including:
- [Domeneshop](https://domainname.shop/about)
- [Domaintechnik](https://www.domaintechnik.at/neuigkeiten/2026/#hosting-mit-erneuerbarer-energie)

We are happy to see **Krystal** crack the top 10 and **Infomaniak** continue to climb the leaderboard! Also, as mentioned in prior years, while **Cloudflare** is listed as the top provider, as it is primarily a proxy service, it is possible that the websites are hosted with a different provider that may or may not be eco-friendly. With the growth of online threats and Internet bot traffic using a security proxy service like Cloudflare is becoming [increasingly popular](https://circleid.com/posts/the-web-has-become-a-content-delivery-network).

### How does .eco compare with the rest of the Internet?

As in prior years, we used the [Cloudflare Radar](https://radar.cloudflare.com/domains) Top 10,000 domains ranking as a basis of comparison. This index represents the 10,000 domains that receive the most traffic through [Cloudflare's DNS resolvers](https://developers.cloudflare.com/radar/glossary/#domain-rankings).

Checking these domains using Green Web Check, we found that **27%** of those domains are using an eco-friendly hosting provider. So at **46%** .eco domains are much more likely to be hosted sustainably.

It's also worth noting that the top 10,000 domains tend to use fewer distinct green hosting providers. They tend to be more concentrated using larger web hosts, which makes sense for high traffic websites. 

Here are the top 5 eco-friendly hosting providers for the Cloudflare Top 10,000:

| Hosting provider | % of active websites |
| -----------------| -------------------- |
| [Cloudflare](https://www.cloudflare.com/impact/) | 17% |
| [Google](https://sustainability.google/) | 6 % |
| [Hetzer](https://www.hetzner.com/unternehmen/umweltschutz/) | 1% |
| [OVH](https://corporate.ovhcloud.com/en/sustainability/environment/) | 1% |
| [IONOS](https://www.ionos.com/environment/) | 0.2% |
 
### What’s next

If you want to check if your website is using eco-friendly web hosting, you can enter your domain into the [Green Web Check](https://www.thegreenwebfoundation.org/green-web-check/). If your site is using a green host and you are using a .eco domain name, you can add a badge to your [.eco profile](https://profiles.eco/profile/certification/green-web-check) showing that. [Here’s how to do it](/news/green-web-check-ecolabel/).

If your site isn’t using an eco-friendly host, consider migrating to a web host that is. Here's some advice on how to [find an eco-friendly host for your website](/news/eco-friendly-web-host/).

If you work for a web hosting provider that is not currently listed in the [Green Hosting Directory](https://app.greenweb.org/directory/), we recommend reviewing the [acceptance criteria](https://www.thegreenwebfoundation.org/what-we-accept-as-evidence-of-green-power/) and looking into getting added.

